  <h3>The parts and what each one does</h3>
  <dl>
    <dt>Python 3.11+</dt><dd>The language. Its standard library already includes SQLite (<code>sqlite3</code>), JSON, UUIDs and a command-line parser, so nothing has to be installed.</dd>
    <dt>graph.db</dt><dd>One SQLite file. It holds the live graph and the journal. SQLite features used: transactions, foreign keys, unique indexes, and recursive queries.</dd>
    <dt>load</dt><dd>Loader. Reads <code>seed.jsonl</code> and fills the <code>nodes</code> and <code>edges</code> tables, then computes tags once for the whole graph.</dd>
    <dt>apply</dt><dd>Operation applier. Runs <code>ops.jsonl</code> in order. Five kinds of operation: edit a node, add an edge, re-point an edge, ingest a node by name, delete a subtree. A delete moves what it removes into the journal.</dd>
    <dt>undo &lt;op_id&gt;</dt><dd>Undo engine. Walks the journal rows of one delete, decides restored, merged or skipped for each, writes the result, prints the report.</dd>
    <dt>dump</dt><dd>Dumper. Prints every live node and edge as sorted JSONL.</dd>
    <dt>Tag propagator</dt><dd>One function: recompute inherited tags for a node and everything below it. Called by apply and by undo.</dd>
    <dt>Store</dt><dd>A thin layer over <code>sqlite3</code>. Opens the file, turns foreign keys on, wraps each step in a transaction, and counts write statements so <code>CRASH_AFTER=&lt;n&gt;</code> can stop the process on the n-th one.</dd>
    <dt>gen_fixture.py</dt><dd>Makes <code>seed.jsonl</code> and <code>ops.jsonl</code> from a seed number. Same seed, same files.</dd>
    <dt>checker.py</dt><dd>A separate program that imports nothing from the tool. It replays the seed and the operations in plain Python dictionaries, then compares that with the dumps and the report for the eight checks.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="n">2</span>The graph inside SQLite</h2>
  <p>SQLite stores tables, not graphs. A graph is two lists: a list of things and a list of arrows between them. So there are two tables. Each row in <code>nodes</code> is one box. Each row in <code>edges</code> is one arrow, written as "from this id, to that id, of this kind".</p>
  <p>The running example is a small data catalog. Ids are shortened to <code>n1</code>, <code>n2</code> and so on; the real ones are UUIDs.</p>

