  <h3>Tags that flow down from a parent</h3>
  <p>The task calls them tags; you described them as types set on a parent that every child should carry. A node has two tag columns. <code>tags</code> is what someone set on that node. <code>effective_tags</code> is worked out: the node's own tags plus the effective tags of its parent. In the table above, <code>sales.orders.email</code> has one tag of its own and gets <code>pii</code> and <code>finance</code> from the two nodes above it. Only <code>contains</code> arrows carry tags. <code>references</code> arrows do not.</p>

  <h3>Walking the graph</h3>
  <p>"Everything under <code>sales.orders</code>" is found by a recursive query. Start with the root. Add every node it contains. Add every node those contain. Stop when a round adds nothing.</p>
<pre><code>WITH RECURSIVE sub(id, depth) AS (
    SELECT :root_id, 0
  UNION ALL
    SELECT e.to_id, sub.depth + 1
    FROM edges e JOIN sub ON e.from_id = sub.id
    WHERE e.type = 'contains'
)
SELECT id, depth FROM sub;</code></pre>
  <p>The same walk, run downward from a node, is how the tag propagator refreshes <code>effective_tags</code> for a subtree: each node takes its own tags plus the already-refreshed tags of its parent.</p>

  <h3>The tables</h3>
<pre><code>CREATE TABLE nodes (
  id              TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  qualified_name  TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,   <span class="cm">-- this table holds live nodes only</span>
  type            TEXT NOT NULL,
  description     TEXT,
  owner           TEXT,
  tags            TEXT NOT NULL,          <span class="cm">-- JSON list, set by people</span>
  effective_tags  TEXT NOT NULL           <span class="cm">-- JSON list, worked out</span>
);
CREATE TABLE edges (
  from_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES nodes(id),
  to_id   TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES nodes(id),
  type    TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (type IN ('contains','references')),
  PRIMARY KEY (from_id, to_id, type)
);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX one_parent ON edges(to_id) WHERE type = 'contains';</code></pre>
  <p>Three rules are enforced by the database itself, so no code path can break them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Two live nodes cannot share a name (<code>UNIQUE</code> on <code>qualified_name</code>).</li>
    <li>An arrow cannot point at a node that is not there (<code>REFERENCES nodes(id)</code>, with foreign keys switched on).</li>
    <li>A node has at most one <code>contains</code> parent (the <code>one_parent</code> index). The graph of <code>contains</code> arrows is a tree.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The journal</h3>
  <p>A deleted row is not thrown away. The delete moves it from the live tables into the journal tables, labelled with the delete's <code>op_id</code>. Undo later moves rows back one at a time and writes what it did next to each row.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>ops</dt><dd>Every operation that was applied, with a running number, its <code>op_id</code>, the time it was applied, and whether a human or the ingest did it. The running number is what "after the delete" means.</dd>
    <dt>removed_nodes</dt><dd>A copy of each node a delete removed: id, name, type, description, owner, tags, the id of its parent, and how deep it sat under the deleted root. Plus three columns undo fills in: <code>outcome</code>, <code>reason</code>, <code>merged_into</code>.</dd>
    <dt>removed_edges</dt><dd>A copy of each arrow a delete removed. Plus <code>outcome</code>, <code>reason</code>, and where the arrow points now if it was moved.</dd>
    <dt>field_writes</dt><dd>For each node and each of description, owner and tags: who wrote it last, and in which operation. This lets undo tell "empty because nobody wrote it" from "a person cleared it on purpose".</dd>
  </dl>
  <p><code>effective_tags</code> is never copied into the journal. It is always worked out again from what is live at that moment.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2><span class="n">3</span>How undo works</h2>
  <p>Step through the example first. The rules that produce it follow below.</p>

