  </figure>

  <h3>Name clashes: two ids, one name</h3>
  <p>Every node has two identities. The id is for the machine and is never reused. The name is for people and only has to be unique among live nodes. A delete frees the name. The ingest then creates a node with that name and a fresh id. At undo time two nodes claim <code>sales.orders</code>: <code>n2</code> in the journal and <code>n7</code> in the live graph. Only one can be live.</p>
  <p>The live node keeps its id and the old node is folded into it, field by field. A field is filled from the journal only if no person has written that field on the live node. Anything a person wrote after the delete stays as they wrote it. Each field lands in one of three situations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Nobody wrote the field on the live node.</b> It is <b class="f">filled</b> from the journal. Example: the description of <code>sales.orders</code>.</li>
    <li><b>A person wrote a different value.</b> The person's value is kept. The journal value is not applied and is listed in the report under <code>not_restored</code>. Example: owner ben instead of asha.</li>
    <li><b>A person emptied the field on purpose.</b> It stays empty. This is why <code>field_writes</code> exists: an empty field that a person wrote is not the same as an empty field nobody touched. Example: the owner of the new <code>sales.orders.email</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Description, owner and tags all follow this rule, and each one is treated as a whole value. For tags that means the two lists are never mixed: either the old list is filled in, or the person's list stays. Tags differ from the other two fields in one way only. They have a second layer, the inherited tags, and that layer is never filled or kept. It is recomputed. Stage 4 above shows every field of both merged nodes; the table below is the first of them.</p>
  <div class="rows">
    <table>
      <caption>merging n2 into n7</caption>
      <tr><th>field</th><th>n2, in the journal</th><th>n7, live before undo</th><th>n7 after undo</th></tr>
      <tr><td>description</td><td>One row per order</td><td>empty, nobody wrote it</td><td>One row per order (filled)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>owner</td><td>asha</td><td>ben, written by a person</td><td>ben (kept)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>tags</td><td>["pii"]</td><td>empty, nobody wrote it</td><td>["pii"] (filled)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>id</td><td>n2</td><td>n7</td><td>n7; the report says n2 was merged into n7</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The live id stays because everything created after the delete already points at it: the arrows the ingest made, and any edit a person made. Every arrow that pointed at the old id is in the journal, so undo can re-aim those at the new id without touching anyone else's work. The mapping <code>n2 → n7</code> is saved in <code>removed_nodes.merged_into</code> and used for the children and arrows that come after.</p>
  <p>This gives mixed subtrees, and that is correct. <code>sales.orders.total</code> was not recreated, so it returns with its old id <code>n4</code> under the new parent <code>n7</code>. The same rule applies however the name got taken: by ingest, or by a person creating a node with that name.</p>

  <h3>Arrows</h3>
  <p>For each removed arrow, undo first works out where each end is now: the same node if it is live, the node it was merged into if it was merged, or nowhere if it was skipped or deleted since.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b class="r">restored</b>: both ends are live under their old ids. The arrow goes back as it was. Example: <code>reports.daily → sales.orders.total</code>.</li>
    <li><b class="m">merged</b>, reason <code>endpoint_merged</code>: one end was merged, so the arrow is put back pointing at the node that took over. Example: <code>reports.daily → n2</code> becomes <code>reports.daily → n7</code>.</li>
    <li><b class="m">merged</b>, reason <code>already_present</code>: the same arrow already exists, because the ingest or a person made it. Nothing is added. Example: <code>sales → n2</code> is already there as <code>sales → n7</code>.</li>
    <li><b class="s">skipped</b>, reason <code>endpoint_not_live</code>: one end is gone. Example: someone deleted <code>reports.daily</code> after the first delete.</li>
    <li><b class="s">skipped</b>, reason <code>child_has_other_parent</code>: a <code>contains</code> arrow whose child now sits under a different parent. The later placement wins. Example: a person moved the new <code>sales.orders</code> under <code>archive</code>.</li>
    <li><b class="s">skipped</b>, reason <code>would_create_cycle</code>: a <code>contains</code> arrow that would make a node its own ancestor. Example: the ingest recreated two names with the parent and child swapped.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When inherited tags are worked out again</h3>
  <p>One rule covers every case: inherited tags are never taken from the journal. After each step, undo recomputes <code>effective_tags</code> for the node it touched and for everything below that node as the graph stands now, inside the same transaction. The cases this covers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>A parent's tags changed while the node was deleted.</b> A person added <code>gdpr</code> to <code>sales</code> after the delete. The restored <code>sales.orders.total</code> comes back carrying <code>gdpr</code>, which it never had before.</li>
    <li><b>A merge puts tags back on a node that has children now.</b> <code>pii</code> returns to <code>sales.orders</code> (n7). It flows to the restored <code>total</code>, to the recreated <code>email</code>, and to <code>currency</code>, a column that was created after the delete and was never part of it.</li>
    <li><b>A person set tags on the recreated node.</b> A person set the tags of the new <code>sales.orders.email</code> (n8) to <code>["hashed"]</code>. Those stay and the old <code>contact</code> is not added back. Anything below n8 would inherit <code>hashed</code>, not <code>contact</code>. The report lists <code>contact</code> under <code>not_restored</code>, so a dropped tag is visible.</li>
    <li><b>The recreated node sits somewhere else.</b> If n7 is under a different parent now, it stays there. The old children restored under it inherit from the new place, not the old one.</li>
    <li><b>A recreated node gets its old parent back.</b> If n7 had no parent and the old <code>contains</code> arrow is put back onto it, n7 and its whole subtree are recomputed.</li>
    <li><b>A node is skipped.</b> Nothing changes, so nothing is recomputed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Work done between the delete and the undo</h3>
  <p>Everything other people and the ingest did after the delete falls into one of three kinds. Each kind has one rule.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Independent.</b> It touched something the delete did not. Example: adding <code>gdpr</code> to <code>sales</code>, rewriting the description of <code>sales.refunds</code>, or creating <code>currency</code>. Rule: left exactly as it is. Restored nodes pick up its effects.</li>
    <li><b>Conflicting.</b> It wrote something that undo would also write. Example: ben set the owner on the node that now holds the name <code>sales.orders</code>, and the journal says the owner was asha. The same goes for the new description and the new tags on <code>sales.orders.email</code>. Rule: the later human value wins, one field at a time. The old value is listed in the report and not applied.</li>
    <li><b>Caused.</b> It happened only because the delete happened. Example: the ingest created n7 because no live node had that name. Rule: treat it as the same thing coming back. Fold the old node into it, keep the new id, re-aim the old arrows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Undo only adds rows and fills fields that no person has written since the delete. It never overwrites or removes anything a person did after the delete.</p>
  <p>The same holds for edits that arrive while undo is running. Each step reads the live graph inside its own transaction, so an edit that lands between two steps is handled exactly like one that landed before undo began.</p>

  <h3>Crashes, resuming, and running it twice</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>One step, one transaction.</b> A step is one removed node or one removed arrow. Its graph change, its tag refresh and its outcome in the journal are saved together or not at all.</li>
    <li><b>Crash.</b> With <code>CRASH_AFTER=&lt;n&gt;</code> the process exits on the n-th write statement without saving the step it is in. SQLite discards the half-done step when the file is opened again.</li>
    <li><b>Resume.</b> The next run skips every journal row that already has an outcome and carries on with the first one that does not. Each decision looks only at the live graph, so the resumed run makes the same decisions as an uninterrupted one.</li>
    <li><b>A dump in between.</b> A node goes in before its arrows, and the database refuses an arrow to a missing node. A dump taken after a crash shows a smaller graph that is still whole, with correct tags.</li>
    <li><b>Run it twice.</b> The second run finds an outcome on every row, writes nothing, and prints the same report.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How long undo stays available</h3>
  <p>The rules above give a correct result however much time has passed. More edits only mean more rows come back as merged or skipped. What gets worse with time is how much of the result the person asking can predict.</p>
  <p>The scenario gives one measured rate: several hundred edits in twenty minutes around a delete of 10,000 nodes. Taking 300 edits, that is 15 a minute.</p>
  <div class="rows">
    <table>
      <caption>edits by others at the scenario's rate</caption>
      <tr><th>time since the delete</th><th>edits by others</th><th>compared with the 10,000 deleted nodes</th></tr>
      <tr><td>20 minutes</td><td>300</td><td>3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 hour</td><td>900</td><td>9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4 hours</td><td>3,600</td><td>36%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 hours, one working day</td><td>7,200</td><td>72%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>about 11 hours</td><td>10,000</td><td>100%</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The last column is a ceiling. It assumes every edit lands on a different deleted node. At twenty minutes nearly everything comes back as it was. After about one working day of edits there are as many edits as deleted nodes. From there undo no longer takes back one action. It merges a day of other people's work with an old copy of 10,000 nodes, and the report is too long for a person to review.</p>
  <p><b>The limit is 24 hours after the delete.</b> A full day on the clock holds about one working day of edits, and it covers the usual late case: deleted in the evening, noticed the next morning. The value is subject to experimentation, because it rests on the one rate the scenario gives.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Checked when undo starts.</b> Undo compares the current time with the time the delete was applied, stored in <code>ops</code>. Past 24 hours it refuses, changes nothing, and says why. The journal rows stay where they are.</li>
    <li><b>An undo that has started can always finish.</b> A crashed run is resumed and a finished run prints its report again, whatever the clock says. Otherwise a crash close to the limit would leave the graph half restored.</li>
    <li><b>The command-line tool does not enforce it.</b> The task puts retention out of scope, the operations in the fixture carry no real clock, and the checks expect undo to run on the fixture whenever it is called. The limit belongs to the full system.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The report for the example</h3>
<pre><code>{
