  ]
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The eight checks and what makes each one hold</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><b>No human change after the delete is lost.</b> Undo only inserts rows and fills fields with no human write since the delete, read from <code>field_writes</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Free name and live parent means restored.</b> This is the node decision in the figure above.</li>
    <li><b>No two live nodes share a name.</b> A clash becomes a merge before any insert, and the <code>UNIQUE</code> rule on the table backs it up.</li>
    <li><b>The report covers every removed node and arrow once.</b> The report is a printout of the journal rows of that delete. Each row has exactly one outcome.</li>
    <li><b>Inherited tags are correct.</b> They are recomputed from the live graph inside every step.</li>
    <li><b>Running undo twice gives the same dump.</b> The second run has no row left to decide.</li>
    <li><b>A crashed undo can be finished.</b> One transaction per step, and foreign keys rule out arrows to missing nodes.</li>
    <li><b>Under 60 seconds.</b> The scenario is about 5,000 rows and a few thousand small transactions. The run script times it and the checker fails the run if it goes over. The work grows in step with the number of removed rows, one small transaction each, so the 10,000-node case follows the same path.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

</div>

